diagnosis_text:
After a thorough end-to-end investigation of the Kath5 topology, no network fault was found. The reported symptom — that PC0 (20.1.1.10) cannot reach PC12 (120.1.1.10) — does not reflect any actual condition in the live lab. Direct ICMP tests in both directions show 0% packet loss with sub-millisecond round-trip times. A traceroute from PC0 to PC12 resolves a clean four-hop path: PC0 → r1 (20.1.1.1, via domain E) → r11 (111.1.1.2, via domain B) → r12 (112.1.1.2, via domain EE) → PC12. Every router along that path carries a correct forwarding entry: r1 holds an OSPF-learned route for 120.0.0.0/8 via r11, r11 holds an EIGRP-learned route for 120.0.0.0/8 via r12, and r12 has 120.0.0.0/8 directly connected on eth1. The OSPF adjacency between r1 and r11 is in Full state; EIGRP peerings between r11 and r12 are established. Both end-host default gateways are correctly configured (PC0 → r1, PC12 → r12) and no iptables rules exist on any device in the path. The redistribution chain — EIGRP routes from r12 redistributed by r11 into OSPF area 3, then propagated by r1 as ABR into area 0 — is operating correctly. The ticket is a false premise: the network between PC0 and PC12 is fully operational and has no fault requiring correction.
=== END ===